PCBoard Internet Collection README.1ST
======================================

The information contained in this file is outlined as follows:

1) TCP/IP Requirements
2) Jump Start Installation Instructions
3) How to read the PCB/IC Documentation
4) Sample scripts that you can modify and distribute to your PPP users
5) Miscellaneous Updates




1) TCP/IP Requirements
======================
  The first thing you will need is a live connection to the internet, either
  a permanent connection to your network, or a dialup connection.  You should
  contact your local Internet Service Provider to take care of this before
  proceeding.

  PCB/IC supports both the DOS and OS/2 environments.  However, there are two
  separate executables because the two environments have completely different
  TCP/IP stack implementations.  The DOS version of PCB/IC *cannot* be run
  under OS/2 and therefore, if you are using OS/2, you will need to use the
  OS/2 version of PCBoard.

  DOS Requirements:
  -----------------
  To get a TCP/IP stack up and running under DOS, your network will need to
  support the "Packet Driver" specification created by FTP Software.  Most
  networks support either NDIS (such as LANtastic) or ODI (such as Netware), so
  your first step will be to enable either NDIS or ODI on your network.

  LANtastic Users
     If you are using the latest version of LANtastic, the NDIS drivers come
     with the product.  If you do not have them, they can be obtained from
     Artisoft's BBS (520-884-8648) or from their FTP site (ftp.artisoft.com).
     Artisoft provides bulletins on their BBS, as well as FAX back support for
     installing their NDIS drivers and getting TCP/IP working with LANtastic.

  Netware Users
     If you are using Network, you can use the ODIPKT driver that is freely
     available on many Internet sites.  You may also need additional packet
     drivers for your card.

  Windows 3.1 and Windows for Workgroups
     Windows can be used by loading the DOS TCP/IP drivers described up above.

  Windows 95
     Windows 95 can also be used by loading the 16-bit drivers described up
     above, but it cannot be used with 32-bit drivers which are not compatible
     with the "Packet Driver" specification.

  Windows NT
     Windows NT cannot be used to run PCB/IC.


  OS/2 Requirements:
  ------------------
  You may use any version of OS/2 from 2.1 on up.  However, there is a newer
  release, called WARP CONNECT, which makes the usage of PCB/IC a lot easier.
  The setup for each is, therefore, different and the following examines the
  differences:

  WARP CONNECT
     To run PCB/IC under OS/2, you must first install a TCP/IP stack.  This can
     most easily be accomplished by installing WARP CONNECT which includes its
     own TCP/IP stack and can be automatically installed at the same time that
     the operating system is installed.  It should be noted that WARP CONNECT
     also includes built-in PEER Networking capabilities.

  OS/2 Version 2.1 thru WARP 3.0
     Older versions of OS/2 did not include TCP/IP as part of the operating
     system.  However, you can also use IBM's TCP/IP for OS/2 "Base Kit" to
     provide your TCP/IP stack.  However, unlike WARP CONNECT, the "Base Kit"
     does not include the SLIP/PPP modules which are required for PCB/IC to
     provide your callers with SLIP or PPP services.  Luckily, you can download
     the SLIP/PPP modules that work with the "Base Kit" via FTP from
     ftp.cdrom.com in the os2/networking/tcpip directory.  The names of the
     files you will need are:

        ppp.zip
        slipmsg.zip
        slippm.zip

  NOTE:  A limitation of IBM's TCP/IP stack restricts PCB/IC to only 8
  concurrent SLIP or PPP sessions per machine.  Also, the SLIP and PPP options
  cannot be made available to your callers if your own system is using SLIP or
  PPP to establish *your* connection to the Internet.


2) Jump Start Installation Instructions
=======================================
  The following information should help you get PCB/IC up and running as
  quickly as possible:

  1) The TCP/IP requirements documented above should be taken care of first,
     but do not become critically important until step #4 below at which point
     you will need to have a live internet feed.

  2) Run the INSTALL program.  Doing so will install the files into their
     proper locations and create sample DOOR program files that you can list
     in your DOORS.LST configuration.

     NOTE:  If you downloaded PCBIC.ZIP, unzip it into a temporary directory
     and then run the INSTALL program.

  3) The INSTALL program will automatically run PCBICCFG.EXE to help you
     configure the system.  The most important part to take care of at this
     point is the IP Information which is where you configure the IP address
     that TCP/IP will use, the net mask for your subnet, the gateway address,
     the DNS addresses, and your domain name.  NOTE:  There is help information
     inside of the configuration program, you can press F1 for help on each
     field.  If you are unable to complete the configuration at this time
     (perhaps you need to speak with your Internet Service Provider to find
     out the answers to the IP setup questions), it's okay to exit PCBICCFG
     at this time and simply re-run the program again later.

  4) Now run the TESTIC.EXE (or TESTIC2.EXE for OS/2) program and give it an
     address such as bbs.pcboard.com.  If it works, you will know that PCB/IC
     is properly installed.  If you have any difficulties at this point, it is
     recommended that you check out the troubleshooting section of the
     documentation.

     NOTE FOR OS/2 USERS
     ===================
     We strongly recommend that you test your installation of IBM's TCP/IP
     stack first by utilizing the PING.EXE program that comes with it.  Then,
     after it is working, you may use the TESTIC2.EXE program to make sure that
     it is working.  Once TESTIC2.EXE works, you should have no difficulty
     getting PCB/IC for OS/2 to operate properly.


3) How to read the PCB/IC Documentation
=======================================
  The PCB/IC documention is available only in electronic (PDF) form.  You can
  print it if you prefer, but as an electronic document it can be searched,
  and as a PDF document, it still includes pictures and other aspects which are
  expected and normally found only in printed manuals.

  You'll find the documentation (PCBIC.PDF) inside of the DOCS subdirectory
  where you installed PCBIC (normally C:\PCBIC\DOCS).

  To read the documentation, you will need to obtain a reader which interprets
  the Adobe Acrobat format.  You can obtain copies of the DOS and Windows
  versions of the Adobe Acrobat reader from the Salt Air BBS (conference 89),
  or via a web browser at http://www.pcboard.com/prod/pcbic/.  The filenames on
  the BBS are:

         ACRODOS.EXE    for DOS (also runs under OS/2)
         ACROREAD.EXE   for Windows (also runs under Win-OS/2)

  After installing the Adobe Acrobat reader, you simply open the PCBIC.PDF
  file.  You'll find the documentation fairly easy to read in this format
  and can print it out if you prefer to read it away from the computer.

     * UPDATE *
     An OS/2 version of the Adobe Acrobat Reader is now available.  Connect
     to http://www.adobe.com/amber/ to obtain a copy.


4) Sample scripts that you can modify and distribute to your PPP users
======================================================================
  In the SCRIPTS subdirectory (e.g. C:\PCBIC\SCRIPTS) you will find a set of
  files that can be used to access the Salt Air BBS.  These same files can be
  modified by you and distributed to your callers to make it easier for them
  to connect to your system.


5) Miscellaneous Updates
========================
  If have you looked at the PCBIC door batch files that the INSTALL program
  created, you might have noticed the following lines:

      SET OS2=
      if '%PCBOS2%' == 'Y' SET OS2=2
      C:\PCBIC\PCBIC%OS2%.EXE
      SET OS2=

  In case you are scratching your head, wondering what the above is doing, it
  is simply making it possible to share a single PCBIC batch file between both
  the DOS and OS/2 versions.  What happens in the above is it first deletes an
  environment called "OS2" (in case it might already exist).  Then it checks
  to see if you are running PCBoard-for-OS/2 (by checking to see if %PCBOS2%
  is equal to Y) and, if so, it sets the "OS2" environment variable to the
  value of "2".  If you did a SET command at this point, you would see "OS2=2"
  at the bottom of the list of environment variables.  Now when it comes time
  to run the PCBIC.EXE, the operating system will automatically decide to use
  either PCBIC.EXE or PCBIC2.EXE based on the value of the "OS2" environment
  variable.

  It's a clever trick that avoids using two separate files and even avoids
  the complicated logic associated with goto's inside of a batch file.

  One more thing you might like to know is, if you run *both* the DOS and OS/2
  versions of PCBoard, you will want to set up PCB/IC so that it can be used
  from both versions.  The DOORS.LST file then might look like this:

                                 USER DOOR            Charges Per    Path to
      Filename   Pwrd  Sec Login SYS  SYS  Shell Os2 Minute    Use  DOOR Files
     ========== ====== === ===== ==== ==== ===== === ============= ===========
    1) PCBIC             8   N    Y    N     N    Y       0      0 C:\PCBIC\
    2) PCBIC             8   N    Y    N     N    N       0      0 C:\PCBIC\

  You'll note that the only difference between the two lines above is that the
  first one has a Y in the Os2 column, the second one has a N.

  Using the above, PCBoard-for-OS/2 will automatically run door #1 if someone
  types PCBIC, whereas PCBoard-for-DOS will ignore door #1 and instead will
  run door #2.  Essentially, both versions of PCBoard can now use the command
  "PCBIC" to run the appropriate version of the door.


